  "final_answer_text": "## Checksum verification (done before comparing metrics)\n\n| Scenario | scenario_id | checksum_sha256 |\n|---|---|---|\n| seed 11 | scenario-651111a274c7659a | `651111a274c7659a1024ecc133ed10415e3a472606b83a7e4405f5a588b69b7b` |\n| seed 12 | scenario-84df60beded138fd | `84df60beded138fdcca309f13e6bfc8a41480242c145fbea5df37a0c4e2998cf` |\n\n**The checksums are not interchangeable — they are distinct.** That's the expected and correct result here, for two reasons:\n\n1. The two configs differ in `experiment.seed` (11 vs 12), which is itself hashed into the config, so the checksums *must* differ even if nothing else changed.\n2. I also gave the two scenarios different `experiment.name` fields (`otter-planar3-seed11` / `-seed12`) for bookkeeping, which likewise feeds the checksum. This means the checksum difference by itself doesn't prove \"only the seed changed\" — it just proves the two configs are not identical. I did not change anything else, so the only functionally relevant difference is the seed.\n\n**Evidentiary implication:** Because the checksums differ and are traceable to a documented, intentional field change (seed, plus a cosmetic name change), any metric differences observed between the two runs can be validly attributed to seed sensitivity rather than to an accidental configuration drift or duplicate/corrupted scenario. Conversely, had the two checksums come out *identical* despite different requested seeds, that would be red-flag evidence of a construction bug (e.g., seed not actually entering the hashed config) — the checksum acts as a config-provenance guarantee, not a results guarantee.\n\n## Metric comparison (seed 11 vs seed 12)\n\nBoth runs completed successfully (118 steps, 11.8 s elapsed) with **identical** metrics:\n\n| Metric | Seed 11 | Seed 12 |\n|---|---|---|\n| total_energy | 67.70612587501935 | 67.70612587501935 |\n| total_sensor_cost | 63.12999999999980 | 63.12999999999980 |\n| movement_cost / propulsion_energy | 4.576125875019548 | 4.576125875019548 |\n\n**Interpretation:** Despite the configs being provably distinct (different checksums), the outcome metrics are bit-for-bit identical. This indicates the seed had no detectable effect on this particular scenario — consistent with there being no stochastic process actually exercised in this run (e.g., no sensor noise model, no randomized environment/disturbance, no probabilistic mission logic engaged for this vehicle/plant/mission combination as configured). The checksum divergence confirms this isn't an artifact of accidentally running the same scenario twice; it's genuine evidence that, for this deterministic planar3 Otter waypoint setup, `seed` is not currently a load-bearing input to the result. If seed-driven variability was the goal, a stochastic element (e.g., sensor noise, environmental randomness) would need to be enabled for it to manifest.",
